A||N||7||jfiero||New Member/rear brakes||||1||
Z||000000||jfiero||09-09-2000||09:00 PM||||I just bought my first Fiero, a gold 86 GT. I love this car. If handles like a go-cart and for its size as some pretty respectable power. The previous owner put alot of money into fixing the poor brakes on the car by replacing all of the calipers, rotors and pads. The brake pedal still had too much "give" for my comfort and I can't get the rears to adjust. The park brake does nothing but all appears to be there and in working order. I was wondering if the adjusting screw in the pistons of the rear calipers is working right. Any way to check? The service manual is fuzzy at best about how to properly adjust the rears. I would appreciate any tips or tricks you have. <P>On a separate note I tried to find any reference to this topic in the archives but the page never loads. Is this feature not working right now or does it just take a really long time to come up. <P>I have several other questions for the future but for now I would just like to make sure the car can stop the way it should before I go anywhere else. Have to keep those priorities straight.<P>Thanks in advance.<BR>John||||reg||1
Z||000001||maninblack||09-09-2000||10:14 PM||||check out the problem I had and it should<BR>reflect the solution you are having<BR>maninblack<BR>good luck||||reg||6
Z||000002||theogre||09-09-2000||11:38 PM||||There is no adjustment for the calipers. There is only an adjustment for the hand brake cables.||||reg||1
Z||000003||jfiero||09-09-2000||11:50 PM||||This is my first car with 4 wheel disc brakes. I have heard the the rears can be a pain to adjust and keep adjusted. <P>The cable doesn't seem to have any excess slack in it and the adjusters are on the stops when the E-brake lever is disengaged. When engaged the handle goes all the way up and I can still turn the rotor. <P>The Pontiac service manual is confusing at best. If I am reading it right I should be applying the brake pedal while ratcheting the E-brack handle to adjust the rear brakes.<P>Seems to me that the adjusters in the caliper piston aren't working. I am wondering if the cables are stretched to a point where nothing is going to happen on the other end.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Linx||09-10-2000||01:42 AM||||I just "re-cabled" my parking brake, and had the same problem.  My fix was to "start" the self adjusting process by putting a rachet on the arms and simulating the motion of the cable; that pressed the pads tight up against the rotors and took up the slack in that adjuster-screw (within the piston).  Then i checked tension on the cable..and theyve worked perfectly since.<p>[This message has been edited by Linx (edited 09-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||09-10-2000||07:28 AM||||Make sure that the two little pins in the rear brake pad line up with the "V" shaped slots in the caliper pistons||||reg||1
Z||000006||Indiana_resto_guy||09-10-2000||12:55 PM||||Make sure that the adjusting arms are installed correctly on the caliper and the return springs are in place and installed properly. Also work the e-brake lever and visually inspect for movement at the caliper. They will self adjust if everything is in working order.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Indiana_resto_guy||09-10-2000||01:05 PM||||Make sure that the adjusting arms are installed correctly on the caliper and the return springs are in place and installed properly. Also work the e-brake lever and visually inspect for movement at the caliper. They will self adjust if everything is in working order. Be sure to check both sides, movement on one side only means bad cable on non-moving side or bad equalizer. No movement on both sides, miss-adjustment, bad equalizer or bad cables (maybe all cables). If need parts as far as calbes and hardware check with FieroDave@webtv.net, he has the parts.||||reg||1
